<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marwan II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/750">750</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marwan II was the last Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, reigning from 744 until his death in 750. He came to power during civil wars and spent his reign fighting internal revolts. He was defeated by the Abbasid revolutionaries at the Battle of the Zab in 750 and killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> in the east.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marwan-ii.jpg" alt="Marwan II" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 8-th century mint masters · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dirham_of_Marwan_II_ibn_Muhammad,_AH_127-132.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…wars of the seventh and eighth centuries the Khazars fought the Umayyad Caliphate across the Caucasus, destroying an Arab army at Marj Ardabil in 730; although <b>Marwan ibn Muhammad</b>'s great campaign of 737 forced a temporary submission, they ultimately held the line of the Caucasus against Islamic expansion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Abbasid Revolution, proclaimed in Khorasan in 747 under Abu Muslim, swept westward with extraordinary speed: in January 750 the last Umayyad caliph, <b>Marwan II</b>, was defeated at the Battle of the Great Zab, pursued into Egypt and killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Arab armies first took Tbilisi in the 640s, and around 736 the Umayyad general <b>Marwan ibn Muhammad</b> made it the seat of an emir governing Georgian lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-tbilisi">Emirate of Tbilisi</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Marwan II</b> sent Abd al-Malik ibn Atiyya with Syrian troops.</blockquote><a href="/state/ibadi-imamate-of-hadramawt">Ibadi Imamate of Hadramawt</a></li></ul></section>
